fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1076", description: some(value: "A SELF-CARRIED SPEC HAS NO CARRIER PARAMETER, AND THE POSITIONAL HEURISTIC PICKS ITS ELEMENT TYPE — measured while settling WI-1069.\n\n`provision_carrier_binding` (kb/typing.rs) identifies a spec's carrier parameter\nPOSITIONALLY: `sort_type_params_as_pairs(spec).first()`. Nothing declares a sort to\nBE a spec or a parameter to be its carrier (059's fact-ban section: no `spec`\nkeyword, no `SymbolKind::Spec`), so the position is a heuristic. It is right for\nevery spec that HAS a carrier parameter — `Iterable[C = …]`, `FiniteCollection[C =\n…]`, `Eq[T = …]`, `Ord[T = …]` — and wrong for a SELF-CARRIED spec, one whose\ncarrier is the spec sort itself and which therefore declares no such parameter.\n\nMEASURED over the loaded stdlib, driving `anthill.reflect.typing.provision_carrier`\nand `self_provides` through SLD (the probe is in WI-1069's delivery notes):\n\n  List           | Stream        | T      <- carrier is the TYPE VARIABLE T\n  List           | FiniteStream  | T\n  FiniteStream   | Stream        | T\n  MappedStream   | Stream        | T\n  FilteredStream | Stream        | T\n  Relation       | LogicalStream | T\n  List           | FiniteCollection | List   <- the explicit `C =` provisions are RIGHT\n  List           | IndexedSeq       | List\n\n`Stream`'s parameters are `T` (element) and `E` (effect row); its carrier is\n`Stream` itself. So `List provides Stream[T, {}]` records its carrier as `T`, a type\nvariable, and `self_provides(List, Stream)` is FALSE — `self_provides` answers only\n`FiniteCollection`, `IndexedSeq`, `Iteration`, `PersistentCollection` for `List`.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, and it is why this is a ticket rather than a curiosity: 058 §3.6's\ndefaults substrate infers \"the carrier's own provision is its default\" from\n`self_provides`, and `defaults.rs` SKIPS any provision the witness classifier\naccepts. Six stdlib provisions are therefore classified as witnesses for a type\nVARIABLE and contribute no inferred default row. Nothing in the suite fails today,\nwhich is the point — a silent hole in a substrate whose consumers are still being\nbuilt (WI-861 onward) is cheapest to close before they arrive.\n\nNOT WI-1069's, and WI-1069 measured why: that ticket asked whether a `provides`\nclause's bindings select a carrier at all. They do, the shape is the witness\nspelling, and refusing it fails 90 tests. THIS is the residue — WHICH parameter the\nreader should read, which WI-1069's description already called \"itself unsettled\".\n\nWHAT MUST BE DECIDED: whether a self-carried spec's provision should record the\nPROVIDER as its carrier (making `List provides Stream[T]` a self-provision), or\nwhether `Stream` should gain an explicit carrier parameter, or whether spec-hood\nshould stop being inferred from shape. The first is the smallest and matches what\n`Iterable[C = Stream]` already says about `Stream`; it is still a change to the ONE\nowner every reader shares (`provision_carrier_binding`), so dispatch, coherence\ngrouping and the defaults index must be re-measured together — WI-860's\nrelation-vs-index agreement test is the instrument that catches a drift.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the six rows above are re-measured and reported after the change; the\nchosen reading is written into kernel-language §5.1 (which currently records the\ndefect with this ticket number) and 058 §3.6; a test DRIVES `self_provides` and\n`provision_carrier` for a self-carried spec and for a carrier-parameterized one in\nthe same program, so the two cannot be conflated again; each test says which case\nfails when the change is backed out. cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nREFERENCE: WI-1069 (settled what the bindings mean; measured this), WI-859 (the\nown-type-param fold this is adjacent to but NOT the same as), WI-860\n(`provision_carrier_binding`, the shared owner; the agreement test), proposal 058\n§3.6, kernel-language §5.1."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-11T15:09:28Z"))
